<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for pyaermod-desktop.app (PyInstaller
  onedir bundle: CPython, NiceGUI, pywebview on WKWebView).

  PyInstaller passes this file to codesign for every binary it signs
  (packaging/pyaermod_desktop.spec, entitlements_file). It only matters
  for a Developer ID build, where PyInstaller turns the hardened runtime
  on; an ad-hoc build runs without the hardened runtime, so there is
  nothing for these to relax.

  The list is empty on purpose. Each hardened-runtime exception below
  was considered and left out, and tests/test_gui_v2_packaging.py fails
  if one is added without being moved into ALLOWED_ENTITLEMENTS there.

  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: NOT set.
    Library validation makes the process refuse any library that is not
    signed by Apple or by the same Team ID as the executable. PyInstaller
    re-signs every collected .so and .dylib with the one identity, so
    they all carry that Team ID, and the release workflow checks it file
    by file before notarizing. WebKit is a system framework. The app has
    no plug-ins, and AERMOD is run as a separate process, not loaded.
    Setting the key would let an unsigned library be loaded into a
    notarized app for no benefit.
    (An AD-HOC signature has no Team ID, so an ad-hoc build re-signed
    with `-o runtime` cannot load its own libpython without this key.
    That is the one case that needs it: a local test, see RELEASING.md.)

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit: NOT set.
    The app generates no machine code. WKWebView's JavaScript JIT runs
    in Apple's own WebContent helper process, under Apple's
    entitlements. CPython's own JIT (3.13+) is off in standard builds.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory: NOT set.
    The usual reason to set it for a Python app is libffi (ctypes and
    PyObjC callbacks). On arm64, the only architecture this bundle is
    built for, libffi's closures use trampolines that are part of the
    signed binary and need no writable and executable memory. Checked
    by running the bundle under the hardened runtime without the key:
    the PyObjC-driven window opens and the app serves its pages.
    An Intel (x86_64) build would have to test this again.

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables: NOT set.
    The PyInstaller bootloader finds its libraries by @rpath and does
    not rely on DYLD_* variables.

  No resource entitlements (camera, microphone, Apple Events, ...):
  the app uses none of them. It is not sandboxed, so file access and
  the loopback server need no entitlement either.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
</dict>
</plist>
